text:
The LNG Group of Assogasliquidi – the association affiliated to Federchimica that represents companies involved in the production, import and distribution of LPG (liquefied petroleum gas) and LNG (liquefied natural gas) – has drawn up a position paper, which has already been sent to the Ministry of the Environment and Energy Security and the Ministry of Infrastructure and Transport, setting out the measures needed to boost the competitiveness of the Italian market for liquefied biomethane, or bioLNG.

The aim of this initiative (Here is the full text of the document) is to promote – as Assogasliquidi itself writes in a post on its LinkedIn profile – one of Europe’s most advanced biomethane and BioLNG supply chains, built by Italy using substantial public funds and a private investment of around 300 million euros in the distribution network, drawing on the best European practices.

According to the association, the measures deemed necessary include:

To launch a technical process aimed at promoting the use of the molecule for consumption (CO₂ avoided), with a view to influencing the new post-2030 EU renewable energy framework;

Unlock the CIC system for liquefied BioLNG, including off-site and virtual facilities (by circumventing Article 6 of the Ministerial Decree of 16 March 2023);

Simplify BioLNG certification for ETS markets (remove the requirement that producers must be certified under the SNC scheme, and allow producers to obtain certification through voluntary schemes);

To further incentivise negative carbon intensity (a reward proportional to actual GHG savings);

To extend the scope of Ministerial Decree 2018 to international maritime transport;

Supporting demand and infrastructure for LNG/BioLNG road transport (fleet renewal, network protection);

To draw up a package of targeted incentives: a 50% tax credit on BioLNG, toll discounts, and an invoice-based discount/incentive for the purchase of new vehicles;

To ensure the stability and long-term certainty of the regulatory framework.

With the aim of capitalising on the investments already made in the sector and maximising the national supply chain’s contribution to the decarbonisation of transport, “these measures are all the more significant when viewed in the context of the future provisions of the 2027 Budget Law, the decree transposing the provisions of the European RED III Directive, and the new regulatory framework needed to overcome the challenges of certifying bioLNG in accordance with the new obligations under the ETS and the Fuel EU Maritime Regulation,” concludes Assogasliquidi.
